      "id": "iprog-4",
      "title": "VS Code Expands Codex Harness",
      "desc": "Source: I Programmer",
      "tag": "news",
      "published": "Tue, 29 Sep 2026 15:19:28 GMT",
      "url": "http://www.i-programmer.info/news/90-tools/19174-vs-code-expands-codex-harness.html",
      "color": "#2196F3",
      "body": "The latest edition of Visual Studio Code is available with more support to keep agents working in your project's development environment, more flexibility for Codex sessions, and assistance on keeping completed sessions organized. This release expands Codex support in the agent host, so you can keep the same coding session while changing where and how you work. The improvement is mainly aimed at people who use Codex with both a GitHub Copilot subscription and a ChatGPT subscription. If you sign in to both, you can switch between Copilot-backed and ChatGPT-backed models from the model picker without losing the current conversation. The team says that if you aren't signed in to GitHub Copilot and want to use the Codex harness just with your ChatGPT subscription, you also have to enable the flag to allow access. You can also move the same Codex session between the ChatGPT app and VS Code instead of starting a new conversation, and interact with desktop apps from VS Code, so long as the ChatGPT app is installed and configured for computer use. This works with models from either your Copilot subscription or your ChatGPT subscription. Another improvement lets you run an agent session inside a local folder's Dev Container. The agent uses the environment and dependencies configured for the project instead of those on your local machine. When the setting is enabled, local folders with a supported Dev Container configuration automatically show a folder menu with a Use Dev Container action. For this to work, Docker has to be installed on your machine. New in preview for this release is a way to keep completed sessions organized. The team says the new feature is designed to keep finished work out of the way while preserving the conversation for later. The way it works is that when all of an inactive session's pull requests have merged, the Agents window can suggest marking the session as done. You can automate this and mark inactive sessions as done after their pull requests merge, and optionally delete them after a separate grace period.  Visual Studio Code 1.138 is available now. \n\n#### More Information\n\n[Update Notes](<https://code.visualstudio.com/updates>)\n\n#### Related Articles\n\n[VSCode Improves Agent Support](<https://www.i-programmer.info/news/90-tools/18985-vscode-improves-agent-support.html>) [Visual Studio Code 1.130 Improves Agents Window](<https://www.i-programmer.info/news/90-tools/19036-visual-studio-code-1130-improves-agents-window.html>) [VSCode Gets Agent Skills](<https://www.i-programmer.info/news/90-tools/18590-vscode-gets-agent-skills.html>) [VSCode Abandoning IntelliCode](<https://www.i-programmer.info/news/80-java/18539-vscode-abandoning-intellicode.html>) [VSCode SQL Extension Gets Schema Designer](<https://www.i-programmer.info/news/90-tools/18370-vscode-sql-extension-gets-schema-designer.html>) [Microsoft Releases Prompty Extension For VSCode](<https://www.i-programmer.info/news/90-tools/17451-microsoft-releases-prompty-extension-for-vscode.html>) [VS Code Extensions Can Now Use Copilot APIs](<https://www.i-programmer.info/news/90-tools/17399-vs-code-extensions-can-now-use-copilot-apis.html>) To be informed about new articles on I Programmer, sign up for our [weekly newsletter](<https://www.i-programmer.info/edit-profile.html?layout=form>),[ ](<https://s3.amazonaws.com/com.alexa.toolbar/atbp/hfdfGO/download/index.htm>)subscribe to the [RSS feed](<https://www.i-programmer.info/component/ninjarsssyndicator/?feed_id=3&format=raw>) and follow us on [Facebook](<http://www.facebook.com/pages/iProgrammer/127140977307932#%21/pages/iProgrammer/127140977307932?v=wall>) or [Linkedin](<http://www.linkedin.com/company/i-programmer>). [](<https://amzn.to/4iJFqTH>)",
      "image": "http://www.i-programmer.info/images/stories/News/2020/Aug/A/vscodeicon.jpg",
      "preview": "data:image/jpeg;base64,",
      "order": 4
    },
    {
      "id": "iprog-5",
      "title": "Why Do We Try To Make Programming Like Something Else?",
      "desc": "Source: I Programmer",
      "tag": "news",
      "published": "Tue, 29 Sep 2026 15:19:28 GMT",
      "url": "http://www.i-programmer.info/professional-programmer/103-i-programmer/5850-why-do-we-try-to-make-programming-like-something-else.html",
      "color": "#2196F3",
      "body": "Page 1 of 2There has been a long tendency in the development of programming to try to turn it into something else— mathematics, logic, engineering, anything as long as we can get away from programming. Why, when what makes software different from all of the above is clearly its strength and not its weakness? In this era of vibe coding, this is even more apparent, and we cannot judge software projects like other projects. Software - we all like to complain about it.  You know— the bugs, the overruns, the cost, the failures the.. Software is a problem for business, commerce, project management and science. Software projects fail, software fails and it is difficult to get it right in the first place and next to impossible to correct when the failures become apparent.  But wait there is something we are ignoring.  Consider a typical software project with thousands of lines of code up front and tens of thousands in the supporting role. OK, so you didn't have to write the compiler and the frameworks you are using, but even \"hello world\" is in reality tens of thousands of lines of code all flying in formation.  Constructing software is by far the most complex of tasks humans undertake even if they do have help from AI. Consider for a moment an example from embedded development - the humble elevator. Embedded design is a good place to examine code because it often directly replaces hardware doing the same job. In the case of a mechanical elevator the call button is just a slightly more sophisticated version of two bits of metal held apart by a spring. The control logic is a few relays wired together to make the motor turn in the right direction and the safety interlocks - more switches and more relays. Complex for a machine yes but compared to the control software that replaces it not even off the launch pad.   \nSo you have a touch screen which shows floor icons, and thousands of lines of code that work out which area of the screen has been touched for long enough to be equivalent to pressing the switch. All of the code that creates the graphics for the buttons, animates the buttons, shows the buttons in different states all comes before we get to the logic of controlling the lift. When we do get to this central task you can be sure that instead of a \"goto first button\" algorithm of the mechanical system we have a scheduling algorithm that attempts to minimize the wait time for the users. And if you push things a little further the elevator can wish the user a good day and add that it was a pleasure to serve as their elevator today. However, my point is that you really don't have to go as far as the speech synthesis the fairly basic workings of an elevator as implementing in software are already much more complex than the hardware equivalent.  **More cartoon fun at[xkcd](<http://xkcd.com/>) a webcomic of romance,sarcasm, math, and language** Software is complex - big surprise but even simple software is more complex than the systems it replaces.  So this is the problem? Yes it is and this is the reason that software has a bad reputation. Even one line of high level code generally has more failure modes than a complex mechanism.  OK, I exaggerate a little but the general point is correct. So what do we do about this complexity problem that we have? We go and look at disciplines that have a reputation for precision and robustness. We try to make software look like engineering but fail to notice that software is far more complex than anything engineering has ever had to tackle. Yes there are amazing engineering projects that make you mind boggle at how it all works out and comes together in the construction - but almost any even small piece of software is more complex. Just think of \"hello world\" again for a moment - to make it work you need a compiler or an interpreter, a complete framework that the language can use, the operating system and all the drivers the deliver the bits as pixels to the screen. This is staggeringly complex and yet we want to liken it to building a bridge.  I'm not saying that there aren't things that we can learn from engineering but it isn't enough. So if engineering isn't going to hack it what about mathematics.  Something in mathematics can be proved correct and this provides certainly. For mathematics to work for us however we have to abandon most of the things that makes programming different from math.  What are these things that presumably are the cause of programming begin so useless and math being so perfect? The biggest difference is the mutability of assignment. When you set A=B you store what is in B in A. This is not the statement of equality that works so well in mathematics.  So we throw out mutable assignment and work with immutable data structures that we have to keep on giving birth to new immutable objects as a way of mocking up mutability! Trying to reduce programming to math causes as many problems as it solves.  Again I'm not saying that formal proofs and functional programming haven't got something to contribute - but it isn't enough. Perhaps AI can help with this approach after all it solves math problems complete with automated proofs of correctness.",
      "image": "http://www.i-programmer.info/images/stories/Puzzles/elevator/waitingelevator.jpg",
      "preview": "data:image/jpeg;base64,",
      "order": 5
    },
    {
      "id": "iprog-6",
      "title": "Robots Build Robots",
      "desc": "Source: I Programmer",
      "tag": "news",
      "published": "Tue, 29 Sep 2026 15:19:28 GMT",
      "url": "http://www.i-programmer.info/news/169-robotics/19173-robots-build-robots.html",
      "color": "#2196F3",
      "body": "Chinese robot manufacturer UBTECH has officially commissioned its Industrial Humanoid Robot Super Smart Factory in Liuzhou, Guangxi. Covering 14,000 square meters, the plant is designed to operate at an unprecedented scale, rolling off a completed industrial humanoid once every 10 minutes, a rate made possible by a highly automated process. VIDEO Last December UBTECH marked the milestone of having built 1,000 humanoid robots. Its new facility has a capacity of 10,000 units per annum. And, according to the video _This milestone is just the beginning. Large-scale industrialization of humanoid robots is right around the corner._ While the previous UBTECH video, see [Robot Army Video As Robots Shipped En Masse](<https://www.i-programmer.info/news/169-robotics/18462-ubtech-video.html>), may have been intended to shock and awe the audience, it was scaled to the point of unbelievability. This video is much more convincing and it seems to be no exaggeration that the facility will be able to produce its robots at the rate of one every 10 minutes. Contrast this with US-based Figure AI's rate of one robot per hour. UBTECH's production line is highly automated and employs its own robots. Assembling a single humanoid chassis requires more than 2,000 screws with 50 distinct specifications. This relies on digital torque stations. Instead of manual parts runners, UBTECH has deployed an embodied mobile logistics fleet. Wheeled Cruzr Y1 and Cruzr S2 mobile manipulators outfitted with 3D vision handle depalletizing, bin picking, tote replenishment, and intra-station component transit. These mobile manipulators dynamically adapt to shifted storage bins and non-uniform pallet orientations, working alongside standard automated guided vehicles (AGVs),autonomous forklifts, and unmanned haulers. Does this mass production mean the robot revolution is properly underway? Up until now the cost of robots has restricted their adoption, allowing humans to be complacent about the prospect of being overrun by humanoids. UBTECH is banking on scale to reduce humanoid manufacturing costs by 20% to 30% annually, giving a sub-$20,000 unit production cost by 2030. Together with the advances in AI, making robots more and more versatile and efficient does alter the balance. But having a robot workforce that can do the heavy, dirty, dangerous or repetitive jobs that we don't want to do, still seems to me to be an attractive prospect. \n\n#### More Information\n\n[Inside UBTECH’s 10,000-Unit Super Factory ](<https://www.humanoidsdaily.com/news/inside-ubtech-s-10-000-unit-super-factory-how-robots-are-building-humanoids-in-liuzhou>)\n\n#### Related Articles\n\n[Robot Army Video As Robots Shipped En Masse](<https://www.i-programmer.info/news/169-robotics/18462-ubtech-video.html>) [A World First For Humanoid Robots](<https://www.i-programmer.info/news/169-robotics/18253-a-world-first-for-humanoid-robots.html>) [Atlas Learns To Be Useful](<https://www.i-programmer.info/news/169-robotics/18108-atlas-learns-to-be-useful.html>) [Humanoids Surpass Human Records](<https://www.i-programmer.info/news/169-robotics/19103-humanoids-surpass-human-records-.html>) [ ](<https://www.i-programmer.info/news/169-robotics/18076-robot-combat-between-unitree-g1s.html>) To be informed about new articles on I Programmer, sign up for our [weekly newsletter](<https://www.i-programmer.info/edit-profile.html?layout=form>),[ ](<https://s3.amazonaws.com/com.alexa.toolbar/atbp/hfdfGO/download/index.htm>)subscribe to the [RSS feed](<https://www.i-programmer.info/component/ninjarsssyndicator/?feed_id=3&format=raw>) and follow us on [Facebook](<http://www.facebook.com/pages/iProgrammer/127140977307932#%21/pages/iProgrammer/127140977307932?v=wall>) or [Linkedin](<http://www.linkedin.com/company/i-programmer>). [](<https://amzn.to/4iJFqTH>)",
      "image": "http://www.i-programmer.info/images/stories/News/2026/Sep/B/ubtech.JPG",
      "preview": "data:image/jpeg;base64,",
      "order": 6
    },
    {
      "id": "iprog-7",
      "title": "September Week 3",
      "desc": "Source: I Programmer",
      "tag": "news",
      "published": "Tue, 29 Sep 2026 15:19:28 GMT",
      "url": "http://www.i-programmer.info/i-programmer-weekly/246-i-programmer-weekly-2026/19167-september-week-3.html",
      "color": "#2196F3",
      "body": "Looking at the news we have run on I Programmer this week, you can't help but notice that AI crops up time and time again. While we've not reported on the stories of AI escaping its sandboxes and hacking into sites that should have been safe, in his latest View Point, Mike James points out that as AI has no motivation we cannot blame it for these breaches. The fault is entirely human. To receive this digest automatically by email, sign up for our weekly [ newsletter. ](<https://www.i-programmer.info/register.html>) **September 17 - 23, 2026** | \n\n### Featured Articles  \n\n---  \n\n* * *\n\n[ Programmer's Guide To Theory - Computation? ](<https://www.i-programmer.info/programming/72-theory/19163-programmers-guide-to-theory-computation.html>)   \n23 Sep  |  Mike James    \n[ ](<https://www.i-programmer.info/programming/72-theory/19163-programmers-guide-to-theory-computation.html>) Computation— we all know what it is but can you define it? This is the first problem to be solved, before we can even start on a theory of computation. [ \\+ Full Story ](<https://www.i-programmer.info/programming/72-theory/19163-programmers-guide-to-theory-computation.html>)  \n\n* * *\n\n[ Functional And Dysfunctional Programming ](<https://www.i-programmer.info/programming/72-theory/6431-functional-and-dysfunctional-programming.html>)   \n22 Sep  |  Mike James    \n[ ](<https://www.i-programmer.info/programming/72-theory/6431-functional-and-dysfunctional-programming.html>) What is functional programming? Surely all our programs should function in some way or other? No - that's not what it means. Functional programming is altogether different.... <ASIN:B081YS81L7> [ \\+ Full Story ](<https://www.i-programmer.info/programming/72-theory/6431-functional-and-dysfunctional-programming.html>)  \n\n### Programming News and Views  \n\n* * *\n\n[ AI Has No Motivation ](<https://www.i-programmer.info/news/245-view-point/19164-ai-has-no-motivation.html>)   \n23 Sep  |  Mike James    \n[ ](<https://www.i-programmer.info/news/245-view-point/19164-ai-has-no-motivation.html>) We seem to be worried a lot about the threat from AI going rogue, and there are lots of examples of it doing just that. However, they are all misrepresentations, as AI has no intent and no motivation. [ \\+ Full Story ](<https://www.i-programmer.info/news/245-view-point/19164-ai-has-no-motivation.html>)  \n\n* * *\n\n[ GPT-6 Astra Scores At Cryptanalysis ](<https://www.i-programmer.info/news/105-artificial-intelligence/19165-gpt-6-astra-scores-at-cryptanalysis.html>)   \n23 Sep  |  Sue Gee    \n[ ](<https://www.i-programmer.info/news/105-artificial-intelligence/19165-gpt-6-astra-scores-at-cryptanalysis.html>) OpenAI's latest generation LLM has successfully deciphered unsolved coded messages from both World War I and World War II, demonstrating yet another area of expertise. Is this a superhuman achievement? [ \\+ Full Story ](<https://www.i-programmer.info/news/105-artificial-intelligence/19165-gpt-6-astra-scores-at-cryptanalysis.html>)  \n\n* * *\n\n[ Cloudflare OS: An Open AI Platform For Enterprise Work ](<https://www.i-programmer.info/news/105-artificial-intelligence/19162-cloudflare-os-an-open-ai-platform-for-enterprise-work.html>)   \n22 Sep  |  Nikos Vaggalis    \n[ ](<https://www.i-programmer.info/news/105-artificial-intelligence/19162-cloudflare-os-an-open-ai-platform-for-enterprise-work.html>) Cloudflare OS is an open source productivity platform designed to integrate AI agents, custom applications and secure corporate workflows into a unified workspace. [ \\+ Full Story ](<https://www.i-programmer.info/news/105-artificial-intelligence/19162-cloudflare-os-an-open-ai-platform-for-enterprise-work.html>)  \n\n* * *\n\n[ Google Releases ADK For Kotlin ](<https://www.i-programmer.info/news/90-tools/19161-google-releases-adk-for-kotlin.html>)   \n22 Sep  |  Mike James    \n[ ](<https://www.i-programmer.info/news/90-tools/19161-google-releases-adk-for-kotlin.html>) Google has released version 1.0 of the Agent Development Kit (ADK) for Kotlin. The ADK comes with a set of tools, custom functions, and OpenAPI specs which can be used to define agent logic, tools, and orchestration directly in Kotlin. [ \\+ Full Story ](<https://www.i-programmer.info/news/90-tools/19161-google-releases-adk-for-kotlin.html>)  \n\n* * *\n\n[ Java 27 Released - What's New? ](<https://www.i-programmer.info/news/80-java/19158-java-27-released-whats-new.html>)   \n21 Sep  |  Nikos Vaggalis    \n[ ](<https://www.i-programmer.info/news/80-java/19158-java-27-released-whats-new.html>) Java 27 brings 9 official JDK Enhancement Proposals, comprising of 4 finalized features, 4 preview features, and 1 incubator feature, along with several notable runtime cleanups and performance improvements. [ \\+ Full Story ](<https://www.i-programmer.info/news/80-java/19158-java-27-released-whats-new.html>)  \n\n* * *\n\n[ Swift 6.4 Improves Interoperability ](<https://www.i-programmer.info/news/98-languages/19160-swift-64-improves-interoperability.html>)   \n21 Sep  |  Kay Ewbank    \n[ ](<https://www.i-programmer.info/news/98-languages/19160-swift-64-improves-interoperability.html>) Apple has released Swift 6.4 with enhanced interoperability, and a new stable, cross-platform way to run and interact with other programs from Swift, from command-line tools to streaming processes. [ \\+ Full Story ](<https://www.i-programmer.info/news/98-languages/19160-swift-64-improves-interoperability.html>)  \n\n* * *\n\n[ Smart Weed-Killing At Scale ](<https://www.i-programmer.info/news/105-artificial-intelligence/19157-smart-weed-killing-at-scale.html>)   \n20 Sep  |  Lucy Black    \n[ ](<https://www.i-programmer.info/news/105-artificial-intelligence/19157-smart-weed-killing-at-scale.html>) The claims made this month that AI could be an existential threat to humans and wipe out mankind within the decade may have dented our confidence and optimism in what the era of AI brings. However, it being an existential threat to weeds in agricultural crops sounds much more promising, [ \\+ Full Story ](<https://www.i-programmer.info/news/105-artificial-intelligence/19157-smart-weed-killing-at-scale.html>)  \n\n* * *\n\n[ Cooley Of FFT Fame Born 100 Years Ago ](<https://www.i-programmer.info/news/82-heritage/19037-j-w-cooley-of-fft-fame-born-100-years-ago.html>)   \n18 Sep  |  Mike James    \n[ ](<https://www.i-programmer.info/news/82-heritage/19037-j-w-cooley-of-fft-fame-born-100-years-ago.html>) There was a time when the Fast Fourier Transform was the star of the show, but today it is simply the foundation that we take for granted. This makes it no less amazing. We celebrate it on the occasion of the 100th birthday of one of its inventors. [ \\+ Full Story ](<https://www.i-programmer.info/news/82-heritage/19037-j-w-cooley-of-fft-fame-born-100-years-ago.html>)  \n\n* * *\n\n[ What Happened At PyCon US 2026 ](<https://www.i-programmer.info/news/216-python/19155-what-happened-at-pycon-us-2026.html>)   \n18 Sep  |  Nikos Vaggalis    \n[ ](<https://www.i-programmer.info/news/216-python/19155-what-happened-at-pycon-us-2026.html>) PyCon US is the largest and longest running annual gathering of the Python community. We provide an overview of this year's event. [ \\+ Full Story ](<https://www.i-programmer.info/news/216-python/19155-what-happened-at-pycon-us-2026.html>)  \n\n* * *\n\n[ NVidia Introduces CUDA GPU Programming In Rust ](<https://www.i-programmer.info/news/98-languages/19154-nvidia-introduces-cuda-gpu-programming-in-rust.html>)   \n17 Sep  |  Kay Ewbank    \n[ ](<https://www.i-programmer.info/news/98-languages/19154-nvidia-introduces-cuda-gpu-programming-in-rust.html>) NVIDIA has announced a new Rust environment for writing native GPU kernels. CUDA Rust will join CUDA C++ and CUDA Python in NVIDIA's range of CUDA languages. [ \\+ Full Story ](<https://www.i-programmer.info/news/98-languages/19154-nvidia-introduces-cuda-gpu-programming-in-rust.html>)  \n\n* * *\n\n[ Julia 1.13 Improves Latency ](<https://www.i-programmer.info/news/98-languages/19153-julia-113-improves-latency.html>)   \n17 Sep  |  Kay Ewbank    \n[ ](<https://www.i-programmer.info/news/98-languages/19153-julia-113-improves-latency.html>) Julia 1.13 has been released. This version improves latency and the Julia REPL now has syntax highlighting. [ \\+ Full Story ](<https://www.i-programmer.info/news/98-languages/19153-julia-113-improves-latency.html>)  \n\n### Book Watch  \n\n* * *\n\n[ Murach's Python Programming, 3rd Ed (Murachs) ](<https://www.i-programmer.info/book-watch-archive/19166-murachs-python-programming-3rd-ed-murachs.html>)   \n\nThis book takes a self-paced approach to cater for new and existing programmers. If you already know other languages, you can move quickly through the familiar to focus on the new as you learn Python faster. Joel Murach and Michael Urban start with an 8-chapter introduction course to Python that gets as far as using functions and modules to organize a program that uses file I/O to read and write data. Section 2 builds on that foundation with additional skills every Python programmer needs. Section 3 dives into object-oriented programming. And section 4 brings it all together as you build realistic database and GUI applications. <ASIN:B0H1ZNKWDD > [ \\+ Full Story ](<https://www.i-programmer.info/book-watch-archive/19166-murachs-python-programming-3rd-ed-murachs.html>)  \n\n* * *\n\n[ Fundamentals of Computer Graphics, 5th Ed (CRC Press) ](<https://www.i-programmer.info/book-watch-archive/19159-fundamentals-of-computer-graphics-5th-ed-crc-press.html>)   \n\nThis book provides a comprehensive introduction to basic computer graphic technology and theory. Focusing on geometric intuition, Steve Marschner and Peter Shirley give the necessary information for understanding how images get onto the screen by using the complementary approaches of ray tracing and rasterization. It covers topics common to an introductory course, such as sampling theory, texture mapping, spatial data structure, and splines. It also includes a number of contributed chapters from authors known for their expertise and clear way of explaining concepts. <ASIN:0367505584 > [ \\+ Full Story ](<https://www.i-programmer.info/book-watch-archive/19159-fundamentals-of-computer-graphics-5th-ed-crc-press.html>)  \n\n* * *\n\n[ Unreal Engine 5 for Beginners (Packt) ](<https://www.i-programmer.info/book-watch-archive/19156-unreal-engine-5-for-beginners-packt.html>)   \n\nSubtitled \"A step-by-step guide to building your first playable third-person game\", this book teaches Unreal Engine 5 via a hands-on game project, progressing from editor basics and Blueprints to AI, UI, cinematics, polish, and packaging.   \nAuthors Zhenyu George Li , Charles Shih-I Yeh and Michael Oakes begin by showing how to install Unreal Engine, setting up a first project, and exploring the editor to build and light levels. As the book progresses, readers will create gameplay with Blueprints, import and animate a player character, and configure controls with Enhanced Input. They'll then develop AI enemies and implement gameplay rules, mission objectives, and win-and-lose conditions. <ASIN:1806105918 > [ \\+ Full Story ](<https://www.i-programmer.info/book-watch-archive/19156-unreal-engine-5-for-beginners-packt.html>)  \n\nI Programmer has reported news for over 12 years. You can access [ I Programmer Weekly ](<https://www.i-programmer.info/i-programmer-weekly>) back to January 2012 for all the headlines plus the book reviews and articles.\n\nTo keep up with the latest news and receive this digest automatically by email, [ sign up ](<https://www.i-programmer.info/register.html>) for our [ weekly newsletter ](<https://www.i-programmer.info/edit-profile.html?layout=form>) and follow us on [ Twitter, ](<http://twitter.com/Iprogrammerinfo>) [ Facebook, ](<http://www.facebook.com/pages/iProgrammer/127140977307932#%21/pages/iProgrammer/127140977307932?v=wall>) and [ LinkedIn ](<http://www.linkedin.com/company/i-programmer>) , where you are welcome to share all our stories.\n\nYou can also subscribe to our RSS Feeds - we have one for [ Full Contents ](<https://www.i-programmer.info/component/ninjarsssyndicator/?feed_id=3&format=raw>) , another for [ News ](<https://www.i-programmer.info/component/ninjarsssyndicator/?feed_id=1&format=raw>) and also one for [ Books ](<https://www.i-programmer.info/component/ninjarsssyndicator/?feed_id=2&format=raw>) with details of reviews and additions to Book Watch.",
      "image": "http://www.i-programmer.info/images/stories/News/2012/NOV/A/IP2.jpg",
